# Pin every text file's line endings to LF, in BOTH the repository and the working tree,
# overriding whatever any given machine's own core.autocrlf happens to be set to. This repo is
# synced across 3 machines (Mac / Windows / Ubuntu) via sync_status.sh's automated
# `git pull --rebase --autostash` loop - on a Windows machine with core.autocrlf=true (the
# default `git config --global` picks on Windows), that previously meant a file edited here and
# a file pulled from origin could differ ONLY by CRLF vs LF, which git's line-based merge treats
# as "every single line changed" - a stash-pop or rebase then reports a full-file conflict even
# when the actual content is byte-identical after normalizing (see the incident around
# 2026-09-03 13:36 in this repo's history: build_dashboard.py "conflicted" against itself this
# way). eol=lf makes that class of false conflict impossible.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Shell scripts specifically must never get CRLF - unlike a false-conflict-only annoyance
# elsewhere, a CRLF shebang line fails outright on Mac/Linux (bad interpreter / syntax errors),
# so this is enforced even more directly than the blanket rule above.
*.sh text eol=lf
